      blocks: [
        table("Risk tools", ["Tool", "Idea", "Limit"], [["Position sizing", "Put only an amount you could lose entirely at risk", "Needs discipline"], ["Diversification", "Spread across different assets so one failure hurts less", "Many crypto assets move together in a crash; it does not remove risk"], ["Dollar-cost averaging", "Invest a fixed amount at regular intervals", "Reduces timing risk; does not guarantee profit"], ["Rebalancing", "Periodically restore your intended mix", "Can cost fees and taxes"]]),
        p("Dollar-cost averaging (DCA) spreads purchases across time so you do not have to guess the best moment. When prices fall you automatically buy more units; when they rise you buy fewer. Over a rising market a lump sum made earlier usually wins; over a falling or choppy market DCA can look better. Neither is a certainty."),
        explorer({ id: "cda-dca", title: "DCA versus lump sum", intro: "Two prices decide who wins: the price at the start and the price at the end. Change them and compare (a simple two-purchase model).", inputs: [{ id: "p1", label: "Price at purchase 1", min: 50, max: 150, step: 5, value: 100 }, { id: "p2", label: "Price at purchase 2", min: 50, max: 150, step: 5, value: 70 }, { id: "pe", label: "Price at the end", min: 50, max: 150, step: 5, value: 95 }], outputs: [{ label: "Units: 2,000 split in two (DCA)", expr: "1000 / p1 + 1000 / p2", unit: "n" }, { label: "Units: 2,000 at once", expr: "2000 / p1", unit: "n" }, { label: "End value of DCA", expr: "(1000 / p1 + 1000 / p2) * pe", unit: "rs" }, { label: "End value of lump sum", expr: "2000 / p1 * pe", unit: "rs" }] }),
        miniQuiz("div-mini", [["Why does diversifying across many crypto assets not remove risk?", ["It does", "Many of them fall together in a crash", "They are all the same coin", "It is illegal"], 1, "Correlation rises in stress."]]),
      ],
      points: ["Size positions so a total loss is survivable.", "DCA reduces timing risk but does not guarantee profit."],
